    "model": "(i) Cellular nature: bacteria are prokaryotic, with no nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles; fungi are eukaryotic, with a nucleus, mitochondria and other membrane-bound organelles. (ii) Structure: bacteria are single cells with one circular chromosome not wound around histones, plus plasmids, 70S ribosomes and peptidoglycan walls; fungi are mostly multicellular, made of hyphae forming a mycelium (yeasts are single cells), with several linear chromosomes wound around histones inside a nucleus, 80S ribosomes and chitin walls. (iii) Nutrition: bacteria can be autotrophic (photosynthetic or chemosynthetic) or heterotrophic (saprophytic, parasitic or mutualistic); fungi are always heterotrophic (saprophytic, parasitic or mutualistic).",
    "prompt": "Distinguish between bacteria and fungi in terms of (i) cellular nature, (ii) structure and (iii) nutrition.",
    "type": "short"
